{"name":"Frontier","hex":"#314a49","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Frontier","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A place in the United States:\nAn unincorporated community in Woodbridge Township, Hillsdale County, Michigan.\nAn unincorporated community in Koochiching County, Minnesota, located on the Canadian border.\nA minor city in Cass County, North Dakota.\nAn unincorporated community in Lincoln County, Wyoming.","An unincorporated community in Woodbridge Township, Hillsdale County, Michigan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Frontier"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frontier","blocks":[{"text":"A frontier is a political and geographical term referring to areas near or beyond a boundary, particularly zones at the edge of settled or governed territory. The concept carries distinct meanings in different national and cultural contexts, but broadly describes a transitional zone between an established civilization and unknown, ungoverned, or contested land. In colonial and post-colonial history, frontiers played a central role in the expansion of European powers into the Americas, Asia, and Australia. Countries such as Brazil, Argentina, Chile, and the United States each developed distinct frontier cultures, mythologies, and policies shaped by encounters with indigenous peoples, geographic conditions, and imperialist ambitions. In the United States, the concept was most influentially theorized by historian Frederick Jackson Turner, whose Frontier Thesis (1893) argued that the frontier experience was the defining force in shaping American democracy and national character. Canada developed a parallel historiographical tradition through scholars such as Harold Adams Innis. Beyond the Americas, frontier zones shaped imperial expansion in Russia, China, and the Indian subcontinent. The word \"frontier\" entered English from French in the 15th century with the meaning \"borderland\"; its use to describe the edge of settled territory is a distinctly North American development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/1800s_Texas_House.JPG/330px-1800s_Texas_House.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frontier","wikidata":"Q1355821"},{"title":"Frontier (banknotes)","blocks":[{"text":"The Frontier series is the seventh series of banknotes of the Canadian dollar released by the Bank of Canada, first circulated in 2011. The polymer banknotes were designed for increased durability and to incorporate more security features over the preceding 2001 Canadian Journey series. The notes feature images that focus on historic Canadian achievements and innovation. Printed on polymer, the 2011 Frontier series was the first series issued by the Bank of Canada printed on a material other than paper. The 2011 Frontier series is being followed by the Vertical series."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/92/Canadian_Frontier_Banknotes_faces.png/330px-Canadian_Frontier_Banknotes_faces.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frontier_(banknotes)","wikidata":"Q3511275"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Australian bushman